course: dictionary
lesson: 3180
title: Enamel: One Word, Three Meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence around them tells you which one to use. Read «Brush twice a day for about two minutes with fluoride toothpaste» and ask what the sentence is about. It is about your teeth, so enamel means the hard, white, shiny outer layer that protects them.
explain: Now read «Plaque makes acid that slowly dissolves the enamel and makes holes.» The clues are dissolves and makes holes. Something that can be dissolved and get holes must be damaged, so enamel is the protective layer on your teeth.
explain: Enamel can also mean a hard, shiny coating like glass, melted onto metal or pottery, as in «an enamel mug». It can mean a glossy paint too. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence: a mug with a hard, glassy coating still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Enamel is the hard, white, shiny outer layer of your teeth.
fact: Tooth enamel is the hardest substance in the human body.
fact: Acid from sugary foods and drinks can wear enamel away, and enamel does not grow back.
fact: Brushing twice a day with fluoride toothpaste helps protect your enamel.
fact: Enamel also means a hard, shiny glass-like coating on metal or pottery, and a type of glossy paint.
exercise: «Plaque makes acid that slowly dissolves the enamel and makes holes.» In this sentence, enamel means | *The hard, white outer layer of your teeth | A type of glossy paint on a wall | A sticky film of bacteria | A hard, shiny coating on a metal pot
exercise: In «an enamel mug», what does enamel mean? | A soft cloth for drying up | The white outer layer of your teeth | *A hard, shiny coating like glass, melted onto metal | Sticky bacteria on a plate
exercise: «Enamel does not grow back.» What does this tell you? | Enamel is soft and bendy | Enamel grows again every year | Enamel is the same as plaque | *Once enamel is worn away, it is gone for good
test: Which is the hardest substance in the human body? | Bone | Skin | *Tooth enamel | Hair
test: What can wear away enamel? | Fluoride toothpaste | *Acid from sugary foods and drinks | Brushing twice a day | Rinsing your mouth with water
exam: In «Brush twice a day ... then spit but do not rinse», why is it good to spit but not rinse? | So the toothpaste can be swallowed | *So the fluoride stays on the teeth | So your mouth gets dry | So plaque can grow faster
